meta {
  name: 03 Payment history
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/centers/me/payments?page=1&pageSize=20
  body: none
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  page: 1
  pageSize: 20
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.payments[0].lines[0].tier: isDefined
}

docs {
  This center's payments, newest first.

  Each payment carries its per-tier breakdown, each line with the price that
  applied at the time. That is the point of storing lines rather than a single
  price: an invoice read back months later still says what was bought and what
  it cost, even after the list price has moved.

  Ordering is by `(created_at, id)`, not `created_at` alone. Two payments can
  land in the same millisecond, and a tie makes the sort unstable — which
  across pages can show one row twice and another never. Create several
  payments quickly, then page through with `?page=1&pageSize=2` and
  `?page=2&pageSize=2`, and confirm no payment appears in both pages or in
  neither.

  Use it to confirm the idempotency behaviour in `02 Create payment`: after
  sending the same key twice, there is exactly ONE row here, not two.

  ## Checks

  - `?pageSize=5000` -> 400. Capped at 100, so a dashboard refresh cannot turn
    into an unbounded query over a long history.
  - `?page=0` -> 400.
  - No Authorization header -> 401.

  ## Scoping

  There is no route that lists another center's payments. The center comes
  from the token, never from the query string — try adding
  `?centerId=someone-else` and note that it changes nothing, because the
  parameter does not exist and is rejected by the global pipe.
}
